course: dictionary
lesson: 929
title: Blimey! Words of surprise
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words show how a speaker feels. «Blimey!» is a cry of surprise, a little like saying «wow!» or «goodness!». You will hear it in British speech, and in places like Ireland and Australia too.
explain: Look for clues. In «"Blimey!" is a British cry of surprise», the word cry tells you it is shouted out, not a long sentence. You can guess the meaning from the feeling, then check it in the dictionary.
explain: Blimey is old-fashioned but friendly and mild, not rude. It comes from the old oath «God blind me», which people once said in shock or anger. Over time the word wore soft, so people could say it without meaning any harm.
fact: Blimey is an interjection: a short word that shows strong feeling and often stands on its own.
fact: «Blimey!» means a cry of surprise, like «wow!» or «goodness!».
fact: «Blimey» is informal, so you would use it in chatty speech rather than in a school essay.
fact: «Blimey» began as the oath «God blind me», and became a softer word over time.
fact: A softened word used in place of a rude or serious one is called a euphemism. «Cor blimey» and «gor blimey» are also used.
exercise: Ruby drops her ice cream and gasps, «Blimey!» What feeling does the word show? | Tiredness | Hunger | *Surprise | Boredom
exercise: «Blimey» comes from the old oath «God blind me». Why did the word soften over time? | *So it could be said without being rude | So it would sound more foreign | So it would be longer
exercise: Which word in «"Blimey!" is a British cry of surprise» tells you blimey is shouted out? | surprise | *cry | old | British
test: Which places use blimey in everyday speech? | Only Antarctica | *Britain, Ireland and Australia | Only America | Only the Moon
test: A friend says, «Blimey, that was fast!» Is the word rude? | Yes, it is a very rude swear word | *No, it is mild and friendly | Yes, it is a word from another language | No, but it is a loud shout of anger
exam: Why is blimey called a euphemism? | It is a long word for a map | *It is a softened version of the old oath «God blind me» | It comes from a school subject | It is the name of a type of bird
